{
  "dom_id": "step-e1587",
  "name": "Example: Identifying the Shift Applied to an Exponential Decay Curve",
  "problem": "The graph below shows the curve $y=\\left( \\dfrac{1}{4} \\right)^{x} + c.$ Determine the value of $c$ and find the equation of the curve.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931416/assets/257c44bbc3762968aee94b2612f489af9f2eda7eb8b5473d5d20f6f3f989cbc9.png)",
  "worked_solution": "The horizontal asymptote of $y = \\left( \\dfrac{1}{4} \\right)^x$ is $y = 0.$ \n\nThe vertical translation $y=\\left( \\dfrac{1}{4} \\right)^x+c$ shifts the asymptote to $y=c.$\n\nIn the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=1.$ Therefore, $c=1.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "result": null,
